(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-anatomy-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"The alimentary canal is another name for the:","choices":["Peritoneal cavity, the space around organs","Gastrointestinal (digestive) tract","Portal venous system draining the gut","Biliary tree delivering bile to the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The alimentary canal is another name for the gastrointestinal (digestive) tract — a continuous tube running from mouth to anus. — Slide 7 (General Structure)"},{"q":"Which organ is the FIRST segment of the GI tract, serving as its entrance?","choices":["Esophagus","Oral cavity","The pharynx","The stomach"],"answer":1,"exp":"The oral cavity is the entrance to the GI tract and the site where mechanical (chewing) and chemical (salivary enzyme) digestion first begin. — Slide 24 (Oral Cavity)"},{"q":"The peritoneum is what type of membrane?","choices":["Serous membrane","Mucous membrane","Synovial membrane","Fibrous membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The peritoneum is a serous membrane lining the abdominopelvic cavity and covering many of its organs, with parietal and visceral layers separated by a thin film of serous fluid. — Slide 13 (Peritoneum)"},{"q":"The gingivae (gums) are covered by which epithelium?","choices":["Nonkeratinized epithelium over dense regular CT","Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","Simple columnar epithelium lining absorptive surfaces","Transitional epithelium lining distensible organs"],"answer":0,"exp":"The gingivae are covered by nonkeratinized epithelium overlying dense regular connective tissue, in contrast to the lips, which are keratinized. — Slide 26 (Oral Cavity)"},{"q":"The most superior region of the stomach, positioned above the cardia, is the:","choices":["Body","Antrum","Pyloric canal","Fundus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fundus is the most superior, dome-shaped region of the stomach, sitting above and to the left of the cardia. — Slide 54 (The Stomach)"},{"q":"The spleen develops from and functions primarily as part of which system?","choices":["Digestive system","Lymphatic system","Endocrine system","Urinary system"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spleen is a lymphatic organ containing white pulp (lymphatic tissue for immune surveillance) and red pulp (for filtering blood and recycling aged erythrocytes), despite sitting in the left upper abdomen near the stomach. — Slide 136 (The Spleen)"},{"q":"The retroperitoneal, shortest and widest segment of the small intestine is the:","choices":["Ileum","Jejunum","Duodenum","Cecum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The duodenum is the shortest and widest segment of the small intestine and is mostly retroperitoneal (secondarily retroperitoneal), curving around the head of the pancreas. — Slide 66 (Duodenum)"},{"q":"The pouches formed along the colon by muscle tone of the taeniae coli are the:","choices":["Villi","Plicae circulares","Rugae","Haustra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Haustra are the sacculations of the colon produced by the tone of the three longitudinal taeniae coli bands, which permit the colon to expand. — Slides 89, 91 (The Colon)"},{"q":"The main pancreatic duct is also called the:","choices":["Common bile duct","Duct of Santorini","Duct of Wirsung","Cystic duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"The main pancreatic duct is the duct of Wirsung, running the length of the gland and usually joining the common bile duct before entering the duodenum; the accessory duct is the duct of Santorini. — Slides 71–72 (Pancreatic Duct System)"},{"q":"The left lobe of the liver is separated from the right lobe by which structure on the anterior surface?","choices":["The porta hepatis","The ligamentum venosum","The falciform ligament","The coronary ligament"],"answer":2,"exp":"The falciform ligament, a peritoneal fold containing the ligamentum teres, separates the right and left lobes of the liver on its anterior/superior surface. — Slide 115 (Lobes of the Liver)"},{"q":"The hepatic portal vein is formed by the union of the splenic vein and which other vein?","choices":["Renal vein directly","Inferior vena cava","Hepatic vein directly","Superior mesenteric vein"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hepatic portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas from the union of the splenic vein and the superior mesenteric vein. — Slides 121, 135 (Portal supply / Pancreas Veins)"},{"q":"The rounded, blind end of the gallbladder that projects beyond the liver margin is the:","choices":["Neck","Fundus","Body","Cystic duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The fundus is the rounded, blind end of the gallbladder that projects beyond the inferior liver margin, while the body and neck taper toward the cystic duct. — Slide 126 (The Gall Bladder)"},{"q":"The celiac trunk emerges from the aorta at which location?","choices":["Within the inguinal canal of the groin","At the bifurcation into common iliacs","Behind the left renal vein at L2 vertebral level","The aortic hiatus between the diaphragmatic crura"],"answer":3,"exp":"The celiac trunk arises from the abdominal aorta at the level of the aortic hiatus, between the crura of the diaphragm (roughly T12). — Slide 139 (Arterial Supply of the Abdomen)"},{"q":"The inferior vena cava carries blood to which chamber of the heart?","choices":["Right ventricle","Left atrium","Right atrium","Left ventricle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inferior vena cava returns deoxygenated systemic venous blood directly into the right atrium. — Slide 146 (Inferior Vena Cava)"},{"q":"Lymph from the abdominal GI viscera ultimately collects into which structure at the start of the thoracic duct?","choices":["The portal vein","The azygos vein","The cisterna chyli","The hepatic duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"Abdominal lymph, including chyle from the gut, drains into the cisterna chyli, the dilated sac at the lower end of the thoracic duct. — Slide 52 (lymphatic drainage)"},{"q":"The hypothalamus exerts direct control over which part of the adrenal gland via preganglionic sympathetic fibers?","choices":["The zona glomerulosa","The suprarenal medulla","The zona fasciculata","The adrenal capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"The hypothalamus drives preganglionic sympathetic fibers directly onto the suprarenal (adrenal) medulla, stimulating release of epinephrine and norepinephrine. — Slides 9, 11 (Hypothalamic Control)"},{"q":"The pineal gland secretes which hormone?","choices":["Cortisol","Parathyroid hormone","Calcitonin","Melatonin"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pineal gland secretes melatonin, which helps regulate circadian rhythm. — Slide 5 (Figure 19.1)"},{"q":"The thyroid follicle is the structural unit of the thyroid gland and stores hormone precursor in the form of:","choices":["Glycocalyx","Zymogen granules","Herring bodies","Colloid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each thyroid follicle is filled with colloid, a proteinaceous fluid rich in thyroglobulin that serves as the storage form of thyroid hormone precursor. — Slide 30 (Histology of thyroid gland)"},{"q":"Chromophobes of the pars distalis are distinguished by:","choices":["Secreting calcitonin","Reddish granulated cytoplasm","Lacking cytoplasmic granules","Forming follicles"],"answer":2,"exp":"Chromophobes, making up roughly half of pars distalis cells, are distinguished by lacking visible cytoplasmic secretory granules, unlike the granulated chromophils (acidophils and basophils). — Slide 21 (Pars distalis)"},{"q":"The pituitary gland occupies the hypophyseal fossa within which bony feature?","choices":["The foramen magnum","The cribriform plate of the ethmoid","The petrous temporal bone","The sella turcica of the sphenoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pituitary gland sits in the hypophyseal fossa, a depression in the sella turcica of the sphenoid bone. — Slides 13, 15 (Pituitary Gland)"},{"q":"The regulatory hormones of the hypothalamus reach the adenohypophysis via which vascular route?","choices":["The internal carotid artery directly","The hypophyseal portal system","The cavernous sinus","The hepatic portal system"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hypothalamic releasing/inhibiting hormones travel through the hypophyseal portal system — a network of portal veins — directly to the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis). — Slides 17–18 (Hypophyseal Portal System)"},{"q":"Which thyroid cell type secretes calcitonin?","choices":["Parafollicular (C) cells","Follicular epithelial cells","Oxyphil cells","Principal (chief) cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parafollicular (C) cells, located near the follicular basement lamina, secrete calcitonin. — Slide 31 (Parafollicular / C cells)"},{"q":"The male gamete produced by the reproductive system is called:","choices":["A spermatogonium only","An oocyte","A zygote","Sperm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The male reproductive system produces the mature male gamete, sperm, through the process of spermatogenesis; the female produces oocytes. — Slide 3 (Introduction)"},{"q":"The joining of a sperm and an oocyte, which forms a zygote, is called:","choices":["Capacitation","Fertilization","Spermiogenesis","Meiosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fertilization is the union of a sperm and an oocyte, producing a zygote. — Slide 3 (Introduction)"},{"q":"The muscle that overlies the bulb of the penis and contracts during ejaculation is the:","choices":["Dartos muscle","Bulbospongiosus","Cremaster muscle","Iliococcygeus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The bulbospongiosus overlies the bulb of the penis and, together with the ischiocavernosus, contracts rhythmically to aid ejaculation. — Slides 49, 67 (Bulbospongiosus / ejaculation)"},{"q":"The ischiocavernosus muscle, active in ejaculation, is associated with which structure?","choices":["The crus of the penis","The bulb of the penis","The glans","The prostate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ischiocavernosus overlies the crura of the penis (the proximal attachments of the corpora cavernosa to the ischiopubic rami) and contracts during ejaculation. — Slides 62, 67 (Penis / ejaculation)"},{"q":"The internal pudendal artery arises from which vessel?","choices":["External iliac artery","Femoral artery","Internal iliac artery","Abdominal aorta"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal pudendal artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and supplies the perineum, scrotum, and penis. — Slide 21 (scrotal blood supply)"},{"q":"Erection is primarily mediated by which autonomic division?","choices":["Enteric division","Sympathetic only","Somatic only","Parasympathetic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Erection is primarily a parasympathetic response: parasympathetic fibers dilate the penile arteries, engorging the erectile tissue (corpora cavernosa/spongiosum) with blood. — Slide 66 (Erection of the penis)"},{"q":"The mediastinum of the testis supports ducts that move material toward the:","choices":["Epididymis","Prostate","Bladder","Seminal gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"The mediastinum testis is a connective-tissue thickening that supports the rete testis and straight tubules, channeling sperm toward the epididymis via the efferent ductules. — Slide 23 (Structure of the Testes)"},{"q":"Within the testis, sperm move from the seminiferous tubules into which structure next?","choices":["The rete testis directly","The efferent ductules","The epididymis","The straight tubules"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sperm pass from the coiled seminiferous tubules into the short straight tubules (tubuli recti), then into the rete testis, and then into the efferent ductules. — Slide 25 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"The prostatic urethra receives the openings of the:","choices":["Bulbo-urethral ducts, distal in the urethra","Ejaculatory ducts and prostatic ducts","Efferent ductules near the epididymis","Ureters, which drain into the bladder"],"answer":1,"exp":"The prostatic urethra receives the paired ejaculatory ducts (carrying sperm and seminal vesicle fluid) as well as numerous small prostatic ducts that add prostatic secretions. — Slides 50, 59 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The Müllerian ducts are also known as the:","choices":["Pronephric ducts","Mesonephric ducts","Wolffian duct system","Paramesonephric ducts"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Müllerian ducts are also called the paramesonephric ducts; in females they develop into the uterine tubes, uterus, cervix, and upper vagina. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The Wolffian ducts are another name for the:","choices":["Mesonephric ducts","Paramesonephric ducts","Müllerian ducts","Metanephric ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Wolffian ducts are another name for the mesonephric ducts, which run alongside the Müllerian (paramesonephric) ducts during development and give rise to male internal genital structures. — Slide 16 (The Müllerian ducts)"},{"q":"The perineum is described as which shape?","choices":["Circular shaped","Triangular shaped","Diamond-shaped","Rectangular"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perineum is a diamond-shaped region between the thighs, bounded by the pubic symphysis, ischial tuberosities, and coccyx, and is typically divided into a urogenital triangle and an anal triangle. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The perineum is bounded anteriorly by which structure?","choices":["The pubic symphysis","The coccyx","The ischial tuberosities","The sacrum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The perineum is bounded anteriorly by the pubic symphysis, laterally by the ischial tuberosities, and posteriorly by the coccyx. — Slide 5 (Perineum)"},{"q":"The broad ligament attaches from the lateral uterine fold to which structure?","choices":["The labia majora","The sacrum itself","The wall of the pelvis","The ovary directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold extending from the lateral margin of the uterus to the lateral pelvic wall, and it suspends the ovary and uterine tube. — Slide 18 (Supporting Ligaments)"},{"q":"The two muscle groups composing the pelvic diaphragm are the coccygeus and the:","choices":["Levator ani","Bulbospongiosus","Ischiocavernosus","Obturator internus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvic diaphragm is formed by the levator ani (itself composed of several parts) together with the coccygeus muscle. — Slide 9 (Pelvic Diaphragm)"},{"q":"The ovarian artery arises from the abdominal aorta at a level just inferior to which arteries?","choices":["The renal arteries","The celiac trunk","The common iliac arteries","The inferior mesenteric artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ovarian arteries arise from the abdominal aorta just inferior to the renal arteries, then descend retroperitoneally into the pelvis to supply the ovaries. — Slide 41 (The Uterine Blood Supply)"},{"q":"Parasympathetic innervation to the ovary is provided by the:","choices":["Vagus nerve","Pelvic splanchnic nerves","Greater splanchnic nerve","Phrenic nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ovary receives its parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2–S4). — Slide 27 (Innervation of Ovaries)"},{"q":"The main portion of the uterus, whose cavity is triangular, is the:","choices":["Cervix","Fundus","Isthmus","Body"],"answer":3,"exp":"The body is the main portion of the uterus, containing the triangular uterine cavity; the fundus lies superior to it and the cervix inferior. — Slide 34 (Anatomic Regions)"},{"q":"The uterine tube epithelium moves the ovum toward the uterus by ciliary action, taking approximately:","choices":["3–4 days","30 minutes","2 weeks","6 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Ciliary action in the uterine tube epithelium moves the ovum toward the uterus over roughly 3–4 days. — Slide 52 (Histology)"},{"q":"The rectouterine pouch forms the space between the uterus and the:","choices":["Vagina","Urinary bladder","Rectum","Sacrum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The rectouterine pouch (of Douglas) is the peritoneal recess between the posterior uterus and the rectum, the most dependent point of the female peritoneal cavity. — Slide 12 (Anatomy of the Female Reproductive System)"},{"q":"The kidneys convert filtered plasma into which product?","choices":["Urine","Bile","Lymph","Chyme"],"answer":0,"exp":"The kidneys filter waste from the blood plasma and convert the filtrate into urine. — Slide 3 (Organs Involved)"},{"q":"Which structure conducts urine from the bladder to the exterior?","choices":["Renal pelvis","Ureter","Urethra","Collecting duct"],"answer":2,"exp":"The urethra conducts urine from the bladder to outside the body, the final segment of the urinary tract. — Slides 3, 58 (Organs Involved / Urethra)"},{"q":"The functional filtering tissue of the kidney is organized into an outer cortex and an inner:","choices":["Sinus","Medulla","Pelvis","Capsule"],"answer":1,"exp":"The kidney's filtering tissue is organized into an outer renal cortex and an inner renal medulla, the medulla being divided into renal pyramids. — Slide 14 (Coronal Plane Sections)"},{"q":"The renal pelvis exits the kidney at which structure?","choices":["The hilum","The renal papilla","The cortex","The fibrous capsule"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal pelvis exits the kidney at the hilum, the same concave medial border through which the renal vessels and nerves enter. — Slides 6, 10 (Kidney Location / Gross Anatomy)"},{"q":"The arcuate arteries give rise to which vessels that ascend into the cortex?","choices":["Afferent arterioles directly","Interlobar arteries","Segmental arteries","Interlobular arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Arcuate arteries, which arch along the corticomedullary junction, give rise to interlobular arteries that ascend into the cortex and ultimately supply the afferent arterioles. — Slide 16 (Blood Supply)"},{"q":"The renal corpuscle is composed of the glomerular capsule and the:","choices":["Glomerulus","Proximal convoluted tubule","Collecting duct","Loop of Henle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The renal corpuscle consists of the glomerulus (a capillary tuft) and the surrounding glomerular (Bowman's) capsule; it is the filtering unit of the nephron. — Slide 29 (Nephron Structures)"},{"q":"The left kidney typically sits at which vertebral levels?","choices":["L3 to L5","T12 to L3","T10/11 to L2","T6 to T9"],"answer":2,"exp":"The left kidney typically sits at about T10/11–L2, slightly higher than the right kidney (T12–L3), because the liver displaces the right kidney inferiorly. — Slide 6 (Kidney Location)"},{"q":"The visceral layer of Bowman's capsule is formed by which specialized cells?","choices":["Podocytes","Simple squamous parietal cells","Macula densa cells","Chief cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"The visceral (inner) layer of Bowman's capsule is formed by podocytes, specialized cells with foot processes (pedicels) that wrap the glomerular capillaries and create filtration slits. — Slide 35 (Renal Corpuscle)"}]};
